{"name":"Lynx Screen Blue","hex":"#2cb1eb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lynx","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several medium-sized wild cats of the genus Lynx."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lynx"},{"term":"screen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A physical barrier that shelters or hides.\nA physical divider intended to block an area from view, or provide shelter from something dangerous.","A physical divider intended to block an area from view, or provide shelter from something dangerous."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To filter by passing through a screen.","To filter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/screen"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lynx","blocks":[{"text":"A lynx is any of the four extant species within the medium-sized wild cat genus Lynx. The name originated in Middle English via Latin from the Greek word lynx, derived from the Indo-European root *leuk-, in reference to the luminescence of its reflective eyes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Lynx_heads_collage.jpg/330px-Lynx_heads_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynx","wikidata":"Q677014"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Lynx_range.png/500px-Lynx_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Lynx_range.png/1280px-Lynx_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Lynx_range.png/500px-Lynx_range.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALynx_range.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg/500px-Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg/1280px-Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg/500px-Lynx_lynx_-_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Profile view of a lynx","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALynx_lynx_-_05.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Winter_im_Wildpark_Bad_Mergentheim%2C